<!DOCTYPE aspectj PUBLIC "-//AspectJ//DTD//EN" "http://www.eclipse.org/aspectj/dtd/aspectj.dtd">
<aspectj>
	<weaver options="-Xlint:ignore">
		<!-- Scope to the REPOSITORY component (the paper's local measurement site).
		     We weave whole repository classes (not the persistence.domain entities), so that
		     get*-named REPOSITORY QUERY methods (getAllEntities, getBasketLift, ...) are captured
		     as the real DB operations they are — while trivial entity getters/setters are kept out
		     by SCOPE, not by a name-prefix rule that would silently drop a feature method. -->
		<include within="tools.descartes.teastore.persistence.domain..*Repository" />
		<!-- REST endpoints are in scope so that IF a workload ever drives them, the recorded call
		     graph gains a real entry-point to repository edge for the paper's step C.

		     MEASURED, 2026-08-05: under the current JUnit workload this include is INERT — zero
		     endpoint records, because no test in the module ever loads a rest.*Endpoint class.
		     The classes named *EndpointTest are misleading: they import only
		     persistence.domain.*Repository and exercise the repository directly. The REST layer is
		     reached only by the deployed app, i.e. the ORACLE path, never the local Kieker path.

		     So audit finding K3 (call graph of 14 edges over 43 operations, demand_scale
		     1.0006-1.0013, propagation effectively a no-op) is NOT caused by weave scope and is
		     not fixed by widening it. The local measurement's entry point genuinely IS the
		     repository method — which is defensible, since the paper's local step is also
		     component-level — and the graph is thin because TeaStore's repositories seldom call
		     one another. Fixing it for real would need a workload that enters through the
		     endpoints. Left in place because it costs nothing and becomes live the moment one
		     exists. -->
		<include within="tools.descartes.teastore.persistence.rest..*Endpoint" />
		<!-- exclude test classes — weaving around-advice into a test method generates
		     $AjcClosureN inner classes that break JUnit's reflective invocation -->
		<exclude within="tools.descartes.teastore..*Test" />
		<exclude within="tools.descartes.teastore..*Test$*" />
		<exclude within="tools.descartes.teastore..*Tests" />
		<exclude within="tools.descartes.teastore..*TestSuite" />
		<!-- exclude logging packages to prevent infinite recursion -->
		<exclude within="org.slf4j..*" />
		<exclude within="org.apache.log4j..*" />
		<exclude within="org.apache.commons.logging..*" />
		<exclude within="org.jctools..*" />
	</weaver>

	<aspects>
		<!-- Kieker's pre-compiled concrete aspect (ajc-compiled, ships in kieker-2.0.0-aspectj.jar).
		     "Full" = execution(* *(..)) with NO getter/setter name exclusion — so a repository query
		     method named get* is measured, not silently dropped. Entity accessors are excluded by the
		     weaver scope above (*Repository only). Writes OperationExecutionRecord(sig, tin, tout, ...).

		     NOTE ON PER-METHOD EXCLUSIONS (audit 2026-08-05, finding K4). A census of
		     teastore-hist-reorder-junior/profile/iter_01 (3,203 records) found the stream dominated
		     by operations that are on NO feature's read path:
		         634 + 342 + 179 = 1,155 records (36%)  Product/OrderItem/Order Repository.createEntity
		         427 + 272 + 222 + 119 = 1,040 (32%)    Product/User/Category/Order .getEntityClass
		     68% of the profile, while the operation actually under test
		     (getReplenishmentForecast, 40 calls) did not make the top eight. The harm is not the
		     wasted disk: these operations dominate the CONTROL set compare_kieker.py uses to
		     estimate machine drift, so a read-heavy analytic query was being normalized by the
		     drift of write-heavy transactional inserts — different cache, lock and I/O regimes.

		     Upstream TeaStore excludes getEntityClass for all five repositories via a
		     concrete-aspect pointcut; our from-scratch rewrite dropped that. Restoring it the same
		     way does NOT work in this harness: the weaver generates the concrete aspect into the
		     classloader owning the abstract parent (the -javaagent jar, system loader) while
		     Surefire's forked JVM resolves it from the test classloader, so every test dies with
		     NoClassDefFoundError before a single record is written. Verified twice, under both a
		     tools.descartes.* and a kieker.monitoring.* aspect name: 4/4 errors, 0 records.

		     The rule therefore lives DOWNSTREAM, in compare_kieker.py (_CONTROL_EXCLUDED_METHODS),
		     which is where it actually bites. Records are still written; they are simply no longer
		     eligible as drift controls. The residual cost is probe overhead on fixture setup, which
		     sits outside the measured method and does not enter its timing. -->
		<aspect name="kieker.monitoring.probe.aspectj.operationExecution.OperationExecutionAspectFull" />
	</aspects>
</aspectj>
